%[text] %[text:anchor:H_BFA240E2] ## ![](text:image:7236)**Application of Branches and Loops: Chaos Game**
%[text] The chaos game is a set of rules that can be applied to many different polygons with varying results. This implementation investigates the chaos game on a square. Beginning at a randomly selected point, at each iteration of the game a new point is selected that is the average of the current point and a randomly selected vertex of the square. Eventually, it will be convenient to be able to easily identify and compare vertices of the square using the map presented:
ChaosSquareMap
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_269E55D7] ![](text:image:5a1a) **Exercise 1**.
%[text] Write a switch statement that switches on the variable `NewCorner` with the cases `1, 2, 3,` and `4`. It should set the value of `NewCoords` to the appropriate vector in each case.
NewCorner = randi(4,1)
NewCoords = [-1 -1];

% Write the switch statement here.
% Run this section to test that the coordinates in NewCoords that you get out 
% are what you expect based on the value of NewCorner and NOT [-1 -1]

NewCoords
%[text] **Note:** Exercise 1 is required in the code [below](internal:M_69327B1D).
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A56487F2] #### Starting the Chaos Game
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_8517A681] Moving on, to start the chaos game, you need to select a starting point. To make the case that the starting point does not impact the eventual results, the code below chooses that starting point randomly. The line `StartingPoint = rand([1 2])` has been left unsuppressed so you can track the initial value, but feel free to add a semicolon to suppress the output whenever you have convinced yourself that the code works as expected. In fact, it doesn't matter if your random starting point is inside the square or not, but it is a bit easier to visualize what is going on if the initial point is visible. Run the code below to start a chaos game. 
ChaosSquare                   % Draw a unit square of a standard size for future reference
hold on                       % Continue drawing on this figure
StartingPoint = rand([1 2])   % Generate a random starting point in the square

% Graph and label the starting point. Note the coordinate shift by 0.02 in
% the x direction for readability. 
s = scatter(StartingPoint(1),StartingPoint(2),'o','filled');
text(s.XData + 0.02,s.YData,"Starting Point")
hold off
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_217931D5] #### Adding New Points
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_69327B1D] The next step is to randomly select one of the vertices of the square. To be able to run this code, you need to paste in your solution to [Exercise 1](internal:M_269E55D7) as instructed in the comments. Then run the code at least once before moving on to [Exercise 2](internal:M_1F5B8F0F). 
hold on
%%\ Code for Exercise 2 goes here

%%\

NewCorner = randi(4,1);
%%\ Paste the switch statment from Exercise 1 here

%%\     

% In the annotation space coordinates, the corners of the square have 
% x = .3 or x = .735 and y = .23 or y = .81. Shift the coordinates to
% account for the transformation by (x-coords)*(.735-.3)+.3 and
% (y-coords)*(.81-.23)+.23 while not going past a value of 1
annotation("arrow",min([s.XData(end) (s.XData(end)+NewCoords(1))/2]*.435+.3,[1 1]), ...
    min([s.YData(end) (s.YData(end)+NewCoords(2))/2]*.58+.23,[1 1]))
% Add the new point to the scatter plot
s.XData = [s.XData (s.XData(end)+NewCoords(1))/2];
s.YData = [s.YData (s.YData(end)+NewCoords(2))/2];
pause(.4)

%%\ Code for Exercise 2 goes here as well

%%\ 
hold off
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_1F5B8F0F] ![](text:image:2cec) **Exercise 2**.
%[text] The chaos game doesn't stop with one step, however. Add a `for` loop around [the code above](internal:M_69327B1D) to continue the game for at least 8 steps. There are comments in the code that give you hints about where your `for` loop should go. 
%%
%[text] This is all fine, but the real reason to play the chaos game with a computer rather than on paper is the ability to repeat many times so you can determine if there are interesting results. Run the code in the file `ChaosGame.m` below. 
tic            
ChaosGame
toc            
%[text] ![](text:image:4000) **Reflect**.
%[text] 1. What do you think the `tic` and `toc` commands do? How would you find out what these commands do?
%[text] 2. Is there a pattern in this image? Why or why not? \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_102C8DB6] #### Modifying ChaosGame.m
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_B8BC162F] Open the file [`ChaosGame.m`](file:../FunctionLibrary/ChaosGame.m) in `FunctionLibrary`. This will open the file in a new tab for you to read and edit. You do not need to change Lines 1-20, but you should read them to give yourself context for the rest of the script. It is extremely common to begin solving a coding problem by starting with example code (or snippets of sample code) written previously, likely even by other people, that is similar to what you are trying to do. Your challenge is to understand how to change or build upon what already exists to solve the problem you are interested in now. 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_9BFD8107] ![](text:image:23e9) **Exercise 3**.
%[text] The chaos game renders very slowly because every time it solves for a new point it stops to draw it. This may be deterring you from running the game far enough to formulate an idea of the final picture. 
%[text] a) Edit `ChaosGame.m` to make the code run faster by adding a conditional to the `drawnow` code on line 48 to reduce the number of times the code will pause to draw the image. The condition `mod(k,ceil(NumPts/50)) == 0` works well. Should you use an `if/then` or a `switch` or a `for` loop or a `while` loop for this change?
%[text] b) Use the documentation to determine what the functions `mod` and `ceil` do and explain what this condition achieves. 
%[text] c) Time how long it takes to render 5000 points. 
%[text] d) Do you see a pattern now? Why or why not? You may want to go up to 25,000 or 50,000 points. 
%[text] ![](text:image:731b) **Reflect**
%[text] 1. Is it easier to write your own code or modify code that someone else has written? Does it matter what you are trying to change?
%[text] 2. Is it necessary to understand every line of code in a file before you start to change things? Why or why not?
%[text] 3. Is it necessary to understand every line of code that you write? Why or why not? \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_EB37F71B] ![](text:image:974c) **Exercise 4**.
%[text] The basic chaos game can move towards any vertex. If you add a condition on the possible next vertex, what does that do to the result?
%[text] Options: 
%[text] 1. Do not repeat vertices. That is, whenever `NewVertex == OldVertex`, select a new randomized `NewVertex`
%[text] 2. Do not move diagonally. That is, whenever `mod`(N`ewVertex,4) == mod(OldVertex + 2,4)`, select a new randomized `NewVertex`
%[text] 3. Do not move clockwise. That is, whenever `mod(NewVertex,4) == mod(OldVertex + 1,4)`, select a new randomized `newVertex`
%[text] 4. Do not move counterclockwise. That is, whenever `mod(NewVertex,4) == mod(OldVertex - 1,4)`, select a new randomized `newVertex` \
%[text] a) By working through each possible option for `NewVertex = rand(4,1)`, explain why the code
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] if NewVertex == OldVertex
%[text]   NewVertex = randi(4,1);
%[text] end
%[text] ```
%[text] does not correctly implement the restriction described in Option 1. 
%[text] b) Using a `while` loop, implement one of these options and then save and run `ChaosGame.m` for at least 5,000 iterations. What patterns do you see? Can you explain why you see them?
ChaosGame
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_8B0CD1C2] ![](text:image:0ed2) **Exercise 5**
%[text] In Exercise 4, one problem with randomly selecting the initial point becomes clear because the first few points may not fit in with the rest. With judicious copy-pasting from inside the existing loop,
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] % Implement the chaos game. 
%[text] for k = 1:NumPts
%[text]   [... Code in here ...]
%[text] end
%[text] ```
%[text] add a new loop to create and throw away at least the first 20 points. Then run your code for 25,000 iterations to generate a clear image.
ChaosGame
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:5bff) **Try**. Predict what happens if you insert the following code after 
%[text] `NewPoint = (NewCoords-StartingPoint)/2 + StartingPoint;` 
%[text] in `ChaosGame.m.`
